  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nInstead of relying on the bilinear formulation for auto-correlation minimization, I'll use a different strategy: one-dimensional line search for patches within the sequence. The central idea is that any optimal solution must have a specific distribution and spacing (like a musical ripple pattern). This method attempts to simulate a sequence of pulses of varying sizes spaced apart to minimize the maximum auto-correlation value. The rationale is that we can explicitly control local structures and allow for more creative optimizations beyond the linear programming approach.\n\nMathematical specification:\nI'll define a parametrized family of sequences of length `n` with `m` carefully chosen pulses. Each pulse will be at a specific position with amplitude and width parameters. Let `p\u1d62` be the pulse at position `i` with amplitude `a\u1d62` and width `w\u1d62`. The starting point for the search will be a variant of the previous parent sequence but with an explicit ripple structure.\nThe objective to minimize is:\n    score = (2*n*max(auto_correlation[0:2*n-1])) / (sum(a)**2)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start from a parent sequence of length `n` (if available, otherwise use an all-ones vector)\n   - Normalize so that all values are nonnegative and sum to at least 0.01\n\n2. Parametrized pulses generation:\n   - Define a sequence with `m` pulses located at positions: `np.linspace(0.02, 0.98, m) * (n-1)`\n   - Each pulse is defined by amplitude and width: pulse amplitude `a\u1d62 = 1` and width `w\u1d62 = 2 + \u03b4\u1d62` (\u03b4\u1d62 is a random adjustment)\n\n3. Centralized search:\n   - Fix the sequence length and vary the pulse positions, widths, and amplitudes using a looped optimization\n   - Use gradient-based information to refine the pulse parameters iteratively\n\n4. Validation:\n   - After each candidate, ensure nonnegativity and apply defensive clipping (0 and 1000 bounds)\n   - Only update the best sequence if the score is improved\n\n5. Phase 7: Final polish:\n   - Apply a simulated annealing process to the sequence to shake out any local optimality\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n   - n: Between 128 and 4096 (including the parent length and nearby multiples)\n   - m: Ratio of pulses to length, ranging from 0.03 to 0.08\n   - Width variation: Small random fluctuations in pulse width\n   - Amplitude: Fixed at 1 for consistent pulse contribution\n\nHyperfine-tuning variations (selected after initial run):\n1. Different width ranges based on the main pulse density\n2. Small sample of pulse positions spaced unevenly\n3. Multiple pulse amplitudes (1, 0.5, 2) as starting points\n4. Bilateral pulse spacing alternated in subsequent optimization steps\n\nHere's the detailed Python implementation:",
